I'm joined today by dr. Leonard Cruz  who's editor-in-chief of Chiron  publications and is also co-founder of  the Asheville young Center  he's a cuban-american writer editor and  psychiatrist as well as co-author of  dsm-5 insanely simplified we are talking  today about the book a clear and present  danger narcissism in the era in the era  of Donald Trump a very very relevant  issue dr. Cruz so let's start at the  very beginning sometimes people get  confused between many of these terms  sociopathy psychopathy narcissism etc  what are we talking about when we're  talking about narcissism  well thanks first for having us on  narcissism is everything that spans from  the normal realm of the healthy aspects  that allow us to become invested in  ourselves  and develop a normal healthy sense of  self all the way into the realm of the  pathologic which at its worst becomes  what clinically is referred to as  narcissistic personality disorder and  that being a condition in which the  features of the narcissism become fairly  fixed immutable they imposed themselves  in all sorts of dimensions of an  individual's life is narcissism or  narcissistic personality disorder is  this something that is that the  consensus is this is something that is a  result of environment or is it something  that is a result of genetics or heredity  there's no real consensus on the matter  I think what you could fairly say is  that under certain circumstances if a  person's wounds in childhood and in  their developmental years are of a  particular nature one of the  compensatory reactions might be the  development of narcissistic inflation so  whether that's also influenced by  genetics hard to say but but more often  than not I think the opinion would be  that it's the environment that shapes  the individual and that narcissism  and in some respects is kind of a  compensation that ends up becoming a  rather huge Distortion like I said  earlier that they would remember  narcissism by itself in terms of  psychological jargon is part of a normal  development we all start off completely  self-absorbed and as we go through life  hopefully the presence of others in our  life become increasingly more important  and become a part of our weave so with  regard to Donald Trump the  president-elect now talked to us about  the analysis and evaluation that you did  in relation to narcissism and sort of  what what you believe to be the case the  connection between narcissism and Donald  Trump you know there is a lot of  discussion on the subject of how do we  explain the phenomenon of this  individual who's risen to the presidency  and also maybe some equal amount of  interest in how do we explain the  response that he evoked in the  electorate and I want a kind of footnote  although it's throughout the book I  think it's important to even footnote  here on the program that it isn't really  our intention or expectation that we're  diagnosing somebody from afar in fact  the American Psychiatric Association  because of an incident back in the  goldwater period promulgated a rule that  for all intent purposes discourages and  all but forbids the diagnosis of  somebody you've never laid eyes on and  had an interaction with okay Gladys song  if you didn't mention it I was going to  next so I'm glad we got that out of the  way yeah yes but but you know that aside  I think it's not unreasonable to suppose  that everybody is trying to apply and  figure out how do we explain this  individual his behavior the  characteristics that we see so with that  in mind I don't know that there's a  consensus that this individual has a  narcissistic personality disorder I  think there's been an appropriate amount  of reserve from clinicians not wanting  to breach that divide and make the  diagnosis but I think even by Donald  Trump's own admission he was quoted in  in a book in response to a book written  by an analyst from Washington who  actually described him it was describing  the productive narcissist and  Trump by his own words kind of signs the  label to himself that he thinks he's a  productive narcissist so that combined  with a whole lot of other things that  have issued forth from Donald Trump  I think certainly raised the specter of  that phenomenon and then of course the  question is I think is just as equally  interesting is what kind of response  does an individual with narcissistic  personality traits evoke in others and  in this case how did he do that in a  very large broad collective capacity yes  so let's address that I mean what  element when when we think of the ways  in which Donald Trump appeal to  different elements of the electorate  that ultimately saw him winning the  electoral vote by as a result of 80,000  votes in three particular states what  was it about Donald Trump's public  persona that may have appealed to others  in a way that's relevant to the  narcissism analysis you know one of the  things that I found perhaps the most  compelling was during the lead-up to his  nomination when we were watching the all  17 candidates on stage at once there was  this one of the debates I don't remember  which one in particular where it was  held but I remember distinctly it was  the one in which he began name-calling  various different candidates he called  marco rubio little Marco and Donald  Trump he ridicule  I mean a Jeb Bush he ridiculed for his  kind of energic a pathetic kind of  weakened state flow and um yeah low  energy low energy Jeb right or lyin Ted  he had a moniker for every your the  thing I was really struck by on that one  David was that everybody I've known and  all men that I've known have in one way  or another encountered a bully you know  when you were growing up either were the  subject of a bully you were perhaps the  bully yourself or you were witness to  the bully and not sure exactly how to  engage and I I watched that scene and I  was powerfully struck by the fact that  there was this presidential candidate  who was basically tapping into something  that is probably very primal and present  in all of us that we  all been treated by the bully in Mon  fashion or another whether we had to  align with them to avoid our own skin  getting scorched or whether we were the  bullet II and you know it was  interesting to watch these very  competent individuals not standing their  ground to the bully same way you might  have watched on a playground so I  mentioned that as a footnote because in  trying to figure out what what did he  tap into collectively some people at  least in the book argued and I think  there's some merit to this that there  were there has been over the last  depending on when you want to mark the  starting point a sort of loss of esteem  loss of power loss of a sense of  privilege you know we've heard a lot of  that bantered around whether it was just  white uneducated males we saw clearly  that he tapped into something beyond  that that but there was a collective  sense that we as a nation we as as  people perhaps who enjoyed some  privilege have seen that receding and I  think he combined that with this  skillful ability to promise things that  most of us understood were improbable or  impossible to achieve but somehow you  felt like your own wounds have been  healed so I think there's something  about that fact that all of us have felt  wounded at some level whether it was  personally on a playground or  collectively when you see the Twin  Towers go down where you see our defeat  in Vietnam and he managed to tap into  that and and then I think like many  people who have demagogue equalities do  he had an amazing ability to promise the  moon which were already seeing him  backing up from when we think about  these narcissistic tendencies or  personality traits in the context of  being president and making decisions  that a president makes what's your sense  of of what the interplay there might be  in what way might these tendencies  impact how someone is as president of  the United States my biggest fear and I  think one of the purposes that we had  behind writing the book and compiling  the different perspectives that the  authors and  had was this was more than just  illuminating the field about Donald  Trump the person this was also hoping to  shed some light on the very phenomenon  of narcissism whether you encounter it  in your workplace because you're working  for somebody who's got narcissistic  traits whether you're married to  somebody who's narcissistic or whether  you're being led as a nation by somebody  so that was really one of the underlying  intents and my biggest concern with that  idea in mind is for the narcissistic  individual there's a very blurred  boundary between the sense of the other  and themselves I mean to a certain  extent the people who have narcissistic  personality disorder  you could almost understand it as they  treat individuals and others around them  in the same way you would an inanimate  object if you know if I have a pen that  is a precious and valued pen and it  suddenly runs out of ink I really have  no use for it anymore and I treat it  with kind of disdain and disregard and I  think one of the dangers that I think  awaits people who have narcissistic  traits when they're imbued with a  political power is that provided  somebody as as something that they need  they may very well esteem them they may  very well over esteem them the moment  that ceases to be the case they may cast  them aside  treat them dismissively and even worse  treat them scornfully and I think we've  seen some evidence that when when  president elect Trump deals with the  media it really depends in large measure  how he's feeling at that moment in time  as to whether he regains the media or he  scorns them mostly it's been scornful  yeah and I think the other example would  be people that are friendly to Trump are  just the best people in the world and  people who maybe once were friendly but  are no longer are really just  fundamentally terrible people and we've  seen that happen too time and again in  terms of the people Trump associates  with very very interesting again the  book is a clear and present danger  narcissism in the era of Donald Trump  we've been speaking with the book's  co-author dr. Leonard Cruz  thanks so much for talking to us about  this oh you're very welcome - thank you  for having us on
 

